-- pending: #161 (effectful callbacks)
-- E2d, a consumer of generators at two rows (SPEC §19 Programs; the kernel program `sum-gen`):
-- `any` runs the generator it is given and answers whether it yielded `true`. It is given `gen1`,
-- which yields `false` then `true`, and, under `to_result`, `gen2`, which yields `false` then
-- raises; the raise passes `any`'s handler of the yields to `to_result`. Written once, `any` is
-- used at `Yield` alone and at `Yield` with `Fail`.
--
-- Blocked by #161: a function with a row is passed as a value, and `any` would handle the yields
-- of a computation it is given. A generator that performs `Fail` besides what it yields is not
-- stored either (SPEC §15 Open), so `pull` does not serve. The kernel types a let-bound `any` at
-- each use by `[let-poly]` (SPEC §24). What it waits on is a callee that handles the effects of
-- the computation it is given, as a generator consumer does: a computation parameter, or `|>`
-- into a handler (#161). Passing the function by name was tried and taken out (SPEC §32 *Open*).
--
-- Once it lands: `(inr (), inr ())`: true, and the error; `live 0`.
--
-- now: 32:41: `gen2` performs `Yield, Fail` and is passed as a value: it can only be called (effectful callbacks, #161)

include fail
include yield

fn gen1() -> 1 with Yield[bool] = yield(false); yield(true)
fn gen2() -> 1 with Yield[bool], Fail[1] = yield(false); raise(())

fn any(gen: !(1 => 1)) -> bool =
  let mut r = false;
  for x in gen(()) { if x { r = true } else { () } };
  r

main -> bool * (bool + 1) =
  let a = any(gen1);
  let b = with to_result[1, bool] { any(gen2) };
  (a, b)
